AssertionError: 'https://github.com/disnana/Nagi/releases' not found in '# Types and completion in VS Code\n\n[Contents](README.md) · [Setup](getting-started.md) · [Extension installation and settings](vscode-extension.md)\n\nInstall the VS Code extension from the Marketplace or a VSIX on GitHub Releases. [Nagi](https://github.com/disnana/Nagi/blob/main/editors/jetbrains-nagi/README.en.md) for IntelliJ IDEA and PyCharm can be installed directly from [JetBrains Marketplace](https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/34891-nagi) in a compatible IDE. Check the listing\'s Versions view for available versions and compatible IDE builds. GitHub Releases provides the published 0.1.2 common ZIP for IDEA and PyCharm, which can be installed manually. See the [setup guide](getting-started.md) for the current asset names.\n\nThe published 0.1.2 common ZIP on GitHub Releases targets IntelliJ IDEA 2025.1.1 build 251.25410.109 or later and PyCharm 2025.1.1 build 251.25410.122 or later. The public API used by the 0.1.2 trust check is unavailable in the initial 2025.1 builds, so those builds are outside this ZIP\'s support range. Users on 2024.3 or an initial 2025.1 build can keep their installed version or upgrade the IDE before installing the 0.1.2 ZIP. For Marketplace installs, check the Versions view for the IDE builds supported by the selected version.\n\nThe unreleased 0.1.3 candidate provides High/Low completion, diagnostics, limited go-to-definition, and an IDE-standard Run Configuration. The plugin\'s High/Low syntax highlighting does not require a compiler; published `nagic` 0.1.11 continues to support manual Check/Run and normal execution through the standard Run Configuration. Semantic assistance requires a development `nagic` built from the same PR source commit. Published 0.1.11 does not provide the assist protocol; select the matching compiler CI artifact for completion, diagnostics, and navigation. Analysis is partial, cannot prove a full build succeeds, and reports only the first compiler error. In a project, the source must belong to the entry\'s import/native graph. Assistance stops while `nagi.toml` has unsaved changes and cannot navigate references without an exact source span. The candidate is not a GitHub Release or Marketplace release; its plugin ZIP and matching compiler are PR validation artifacts. See the [JetBrains guide](../../editors/jetbrains-nagi/README.en.md) for the current constraints.\n\nGeneration retention is also under test with the development compiler from the same PR source and applies only to IDE-managed runs. Published `nagic` 0.1.11 supports normal CLI Check/Run but does not automatically reclaim generations. CLI generations are retained, and the shared Cargo cache is separate. Candidate cleanup protects a latest generation only after a recorded successful native run, active generations, generations referenced by all apps\' input snapshots, and the last-good generation after failure. Compile success alone is not run success; managed generations with unknown input metadata are retained. There is no strict generation-count or disk-capacity limit, and a referenced generation can survive until an additional successful sweep after its owner is removed. The temporary compiler 0.1.12 candidate was cancelled. JetBrains 0.1.3 and its matching compiler remain unreleased candidates. See [project configuration](projects.md) for the distinction between generations and the Cargo cache.\n\nUse Nagi extension 0.1.13 and `nagic` 0.1.11 after its availability is confirmed in the official 0.1.11 release record. This guide targets the 0.1.11 release. Open the repository in VS Code, or save the example below in your own folder.\n\n## Indentation support\n\nExtension 0.1.12 and later support indentation after headers such as `def main():`, else/case alignment, and closing delimiter alignment in multiline expressions.\n\nTyping the final colon of `else:` or `case ...:` aligns it with its enclosing `if` or `match`. The default is